@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:global.pattern.modal a ds:Pattern;
    ds:name "Modal";
    ds:summary "A modal is a focused container that sits on top of the main view, requiring users to interact with it before returning to that view. Its primary purpose is to capture a user’s full attention for a specific, self-contained task while keeping their current workspace visible (though inactive) in the background.\n\n";
    ds:anatomyDsl "---\n# Authored 2026-09-13 from @canonical/react-ds-global\n# src/lib/pattern/Modal/styles.css on .ds.modal, which IS the <dialog> element:\n# the stylesheet's own mapping note is explicit that the backdrop is ::backdrop\n# (painted by the native top layer, with no DOM node) and that the former\n# \"dialog container\" wrapper is the dialog itself, so the sections hang directly\n# off the root here.\nnode:\n  uri: global.pattern.modal\n  styles:\n    # Declared only on [open], because a closed <dialog> is display: none by\n    # user-agent rule and an unconditional display would leave it visible.\n    layout.type: flex\n    layout.direction: column\n    # The content pane is the only scroller.\n    layout.overflow: hidden\n    # Padding stays on the header, content and footer, never here, so a click\n    # landing on the dialog box itself can only be a backdrop click.\n    spacing.internal.block.start: 0\n    spacing.internal.block.end: 0\n    spacing.internal.inline.start: 0\n    spacing.internal.inline.end: 0\n    # The token layer defines a `.modal` surface, matched by the component's own\n    # `ds modal` class, so the dialog paints at base surface depth even when\n    # opened from a nested .surface. The background channel is always emitted\n    # there and is read bare; the text channel is emitted only on\n    # polarity-inverting surfaces, so it falls through to the ambient page text.\n    appearance.background: surface.color.background\n    typography.color: [surface.color.text, color.text]\n    # The user-agent stylesheet gives <dialog> a medium solid border, so `none`\n    # is written explicitly rather than left undeclared.\n    appearance.border.style: none\n    appearance.radius: dimension.radius.medium\n    # `max-block-size: 80vh` — bound on the max-height key, the roster's nearest\n    # (size.max.block takes a token only). `inline-size` is a clamp() over\n    # --modal-min-inline-size (50ch) and --modal-max-inline-size (80vw), which\n    # design-tokens has no size/modal token for; size.inline takes a token only,\n    # so the clamp is not bound.\n    size.max.height: 80vh\n  edges:\n    - node:\n        role: backdrop\n        styles:\n          # ::backdrop, not a DOM node: it has no surface channel to read, so the\n          # backdrop colour is a component token of its own. The\n          # backdrop-filter blur reads --modal-backdrop-blur (0) and no roster\n          # key takes a filter.\n          appearance.background: color.backdrop\n      relation:\n        cardinality: \"1\"\n    - node:\n        uri: global.subcomponent.modal-header\n      relation:\n        cardinality: \"0..1\"\n        slotName: header\n    - node:\n        uri: global.subcomponent.modal-content\n      relation:\n        cardinality: \"1\"\n        slotName: default\n    - node:\n        uri: global.subcomponent.modal-footer\n      relation:\n        cardinality: \"0..1\"\n        slotName: footer";
    ds:anatomyClassic "https://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=5559-717&t=P91QYCdXnFopyMm0-1";
    ds:usage "Modals are intended for focused, interruptive interactions. They are suitable for moments where the user must complete an action or understand contextual information before returning to their current flow.\n\n**When to use**\n\n- When a confirmation is needed before completing or canceling an action, modals should be used. They are well suited for this because they capture full attention and require an explicit decision from the user. \n- When a form is displayed on a website rather than an app. On apps, forms should generally use side panels or full pages instead. If a form requires a nested step, layer side panels rather than escalating to a modal.\n- When providing extra context or instructions that are supplementary to the main task. For example, explaining search syntax rules or describing how to format a cron job expression.\n- When the system needs to interrupt the user with a warning, such as when unsaved changes are about to be lost or a critical condition requires acknowledgment.\n- When a side panel triggers a secondary step that involves a small, self-contained form that is not a fully independent entity in the software. For example, selecting an image for an LXD container. This is only appropriate when the nested step is minor. If the nested step is a complete form that already exists elsewhere in the UI, use a nested side panel for consistency.\n- When a side panel is displaying an item that has associated entities, and the main listing page cannot be filtered to show only the related ones. For example, when an instance is related to several profiles and there is no way to filter the profiles listing page down to only the associated ones. If the item is displayed on a full page instead of a side panel, open the associated list in a side panel rather than a modal.\n\n\n\n**When not to use**\n\n- When a modal would need to trigger a side panel as a secondary step. A side panel can trigger a modal, but a modal cannot trigger a side panel.\n  - Incorrect: A modal form that opens a side panel when the user needs to complete a prerequisite step.\n  - Remediation: Restructure the flow so that the initial container is a side panel, which can then trigger a modal for any smaller interruption if needed.\n- When a form is required to complete an app workflow. Use side panels or full pages instead, which keep the user oriented within the application structure. [This is also the case for forms with only one field.]\n  - Incorrect: Opening a modal to fill out a multi-field entity creation form inside an app.\n  - Remediation: Use a side panel or a dedicated page. If a nested step is required, layer a second side panel on top of the first.\n- When displaying static information about an entity, such as a read-only detail view. Static content does not require the level of interruption a modal creates.\n  - Incorrect: A modal that opens to show the details of an entity with no actions available.\n  - Remediation: Use a side panel to display entity details while keeping the background context visible.\n- When the goal is to guide a first-time user through the interface in a tour or onboarding flow.\n  - Incorrect: A modal sequence that blocks the UI to walk the user through each feature step by step.\n  - Remediation: Use popovers, which allow the user to interact with the UI and exit the flow at any point.\n- When a lighter, non-interruptive overlay could serve the same purpose. Modals block the entire view and force a decision, while popovers allow continued interaction with the page.\n  - Incorrect: Using a modal to show a tooltip-style explanation of a field while the user is filling out a form.\n  - Remediation: Use a popover, which surfaces the information without interrupting the user's flow.\n- When providing feedback on the outcome of an action, whether successful or not.\n  - Incorrect: A modal that appears after completing an action to confirm it was done successfully.\n  - Remediation: Use a toast notification, which communicates the result without requiring any interaction from the user.";
    ds:guidelines "Web : It is recommended to use a backdrop for the modal if the browser allows for it\n\nConfirmation modals, constructive or destructive, should include a description of the consequences. The button used in the Modal footer should reflect the modifier context, for instance a modal that confirms a [Destructive](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-aygAVqOfFj/_rui-VZYLmDJC1O)  action should use a [Destructive](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-aygAVqOfFj/_rui-VZYLmDJC1O)  [Button](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-20dWwIHYhx/_rui-8SdtbQUhTr) .\n\nIf the action is destructive, a [Intentional friction](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-GnfOU3MM-3/_rui-jBkiiurLeQ)  should be considered.";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:documentationStage ds:tag.needsdefinition;
